<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kalachuris of Kalyani</h1><p class="st-meta">1156 CE – 1184 CE · capital Kalyani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived dynasty that seized the Western Chalukya capital of Kalyani, remembered for the rise of the Lingayat movement under Basava.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kalachuris were feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Chalukyas of Kalyani</a> holding territory in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> power weakened, their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala II</a>, a commander and minister of the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-iii">Taila III</a>, took control of the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a> around 1156 and by 1162 proclaimed himself emperor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasty ruled much of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> realm in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Its reign coincided with the career of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, a minister at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala</a>'s court, who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Virashaiva</a> or Lingayat reform movement, rejecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> hierarchy and temple ritual and promoting vachana poetry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>. Tensions between the movement and orthodox society led to unrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijjala-ii">Bijjala</a> was killed around 1167, and his sons ruled weakly amid revolts. In 1184 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-iv">Someshvara IV</a> recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basavakalyan">Kalyani</a>, ending Kalachuri rule. The region soon passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Seuna Yadava dynasty</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> tradition endured.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kalachuris-of-kalyani.jpg" alt="Kalachuris of Kalyani" width="320" height="170"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalachuris_of_Kalyana._temp_Bijjala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1156 CE – 1184 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kalyani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bijjala II, Sovideva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kannada</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Southern Kalachuris</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1170&amp;focus=kalachuris-of-kalyani" aria-label="Kalachuris of Kalyani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7043 1330 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kalachuris of Kalyani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7363 1586L7088 1770"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1770h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7363 1586h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7363 1586h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kalyani. <a href="/atlas?year=1170&amp;focus=kalachuris-of-kalyani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
